I found the bug: my color-role assignment picked the wrong "noise" color in demo 2 (it chose 8 instead of 0). The noise color is 0 in all instances; the marker is the rarest non-background color. The rule is: take the marker shape as a template, and stamp it (in marker color) at every position where exactly the template cells are all noise-colored. I verified this explains all stamps in both demos.

```python
def transform(grid):
    from collections import Counter
    R, C = len(grid), len(grid[0])
    cnt = Counter(v for row in grid for v in row)
    bg = cnt.most_common(1)[0][0]
    nonbg = [v for v in cnt if v != bg]
    if not nonbg:
        return [r[:] for r in grid]
    marker = min(nonbg, key=lambda v: cnt[v])
    if 0 in cnt and 0 != bg:
        noise = 0
    else:
        rest = [v for v in nonbg if v != marker]
        noise = max(rest, key=lambda v: cnt[v]) if rest else None
    cells = [(i, j) for i in range(R) for j in range(C) if grid[i][j] == marker]
    if not cells or noise is None:
        return [r[:] for r in grid]
    r0 = min(i for i, j in cells)
    c0 = min(j for i, j in cells)
    tpl = [(i - r0, j - c0) for i, j in cells]
    H = max(i for i, j in tpl) + 1
    W = max(j for i, j in tpl) + 1
    out = [r[:] for r in grid]
    for a in range(R - H + 1):
        for b in range(C - W + 1):
            if all(grid[a + i][b + j] == noise for i, j in tpl):
                for i, j in tpl:
                    out[a + i][b + j] = marker
    return out
```

